{"event_id":"EVT-000077","timestamp_utc":"2026-08-31T05:36:56.041Z","session_id":"SESSION-BATCH-20260831T031501646457Z","run_id":"RUN-IMO-110-20260831T053422309042Z","requirement_id":"IMO-110","event_type":"validator_feedback_possible_reversal","iteration":4,"prior_feedback_history":["Add SHACL Core property constraints for nltl:entireCargoTankLengthDoubleBottomProtectionStatus and nltl:wingTankProtectionStatus with sh:datatype xsd:boolean, sh:minCount 1, and the appropriate single-value cardinality. Then enforce the conditional outcome with positive checks that each status is exactly true; the current three-query NOT EXISTS/!= construction lacks datatype/cardinality validation and can accept malformed non-boolean status data. Also avoid making oil-tanker applicability depend on both nltl:shipType = \"oil_tanker\" and rdf:type nltl:oilTanker unless both are independently required; use the canonical oil-tanker representation without imposing a redundant intersection.","Revise the applicability encoding to satisfy the COMPLETE directConstraintTerms obligation for nltl:oilTanker: the candidate currently tests only shipType = \"oil_tanker\" and omits the supplied canonical oil-tanker concept. Use nltl:oilTanker as the canonical oil-tanker representation without redundantly requiring both rdf:type nltl:oilTanker and shipType = \"oil_tanker\"; retain shipType only if independently required. Preserve the existing boolean, cardinality, category, deadweight-unit, threshold, and date checks.","Add the required canonical nltl:shipType applicability input on the ship, with sh:datatype xsd:string, sh:minCount 1, and sh:maxCount 1, and require its value to be \"oil_tanker\" in the conditional SPARQL check. Retain rdf:type nltl:oilTanker as the canonical oil-tanker representation without treating it as a substitute for the independently declared shipType obligation. Also revise the outcome query to enforce each protection status as exactly true using explicit positive true checks; do not rely only on the current combined != true expression."],"current_feedback":"The outcome constraints are unconditional: sh:minCount 1 and sh:hasValue true on both protection-status properties require every targeted ship to have both statuses, even when the applicability conditions are false. Move their existence and exact-true enforcement into the conditional applicability check, so only a ship satisfying Category A/B, shipType = \"oil_tanker\", rdf:type nltl:oilTanker, deadweight < 5000 metric tonnes, and constructionDate >= 2017-01-01 is required to have both statuses exactly true. Keep the required ship-level datatype, cardinality, deadweight unit, threshold, category, date, and shipType constraints. Revise the SPARQL violation logic to enforce each conditional outcome explicitly and avoid relying on unconditional positive property shapes; retain the latest prior instruction requiring both the canonical oil-tanker class representation and the independently required shipType value.","blocking":false,"semantic_attempt_consumed":false}
